<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">White Nile</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The White <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> is the longer of the two main branches of the Nile, flowing north from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> region through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a> to meet the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>. The Shilluk kingdom lay along its banks, and in the 19th century Turco-Egyptian merchants opened it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trades</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/white-nile.jpg" alt="White Nile" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User DEMOSH on flickr.com · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sudan_Juba_bridge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The White Nile is the less voluminous but longer, wider and shallower of the two main tributaries of the Nile, the larger being the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a>. Its name comes from the pale clay sediment in its water. In the strict sense the name belongs to the river formed at Lake No by the meeting of the Bahr al Jabal and the Bahr el Ghazal. In a wider sense it covers all the stretches from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a> to the merger with the Blue Nile, the most remote headwaters lying 3,700 km from that point.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagera-river">Kagera River</a> is the longest feeder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a>, and the source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> is variously placed at the Ruvyironza in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a> or the Nyabarongo, which flows from the Nyungwe Forest in Rwanda. The two meet near Rusumo Falls. The river leaves Lake Victoria at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinja">Jinja</a> in Uganda as the Victoria Nile, passes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-kyoga">Lake Kyoga</a>, and is squeezed to a passage seven metres wide at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murchison-falls">Murchison Falls</a> before it enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-albert">Lake Albert</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-albert">Lake Albert</a> to the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a> the river is the Albert Nile. At Nimule it becomes the Mountain Nile, Baḥr al-Jabal, which runs through rapids to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> plain and the vast Sudd swamp. From Lake No the White Nile flows east, very gently sloping through swamps and lagoons, to the mouth of the Sobat, whose sediment in flood adds to its pale colour. It then runs past Malakal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fashoda">Kodok</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>, where it joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a> to form the River Nile.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fashoda">Kodok</a> was the site of the Fashoda Incident of 1898, which marked the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scramble-for-africa">Scramble for Africa</a>. Karuma Bridge, built in 1963 to help the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> industry, later served as a convoy assembly point during the insurgency of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lords-resistance-army">Lord's Resistance Army</a>. On 28–29 April 1994 about 250,000 Rwandans crossed the bridge at Rusumo Falls into Tanzania in 24 hours, which the United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees called the largest and fastest refugee exodus in modern times.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river is a navigable waterway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-albert">Lake Albert</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a> by way of the Jebel Aulia Dam, although the section between Juba and Uganda would need upgrading. At some seasons boats can reach Gambela in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and Wau in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a>. The Nalubaale and Kiira power stations stand at the outlet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a>, and the Bujagali Power Station at Bujagali Falls. A Sudanese state takes its name from the river.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “White Nile” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its capital Soba, near the confluence of the Blue and <b>White Niles</b>, was destroyed by an alliance of the Funj and the Arab tribes of the Qawasma led by Abdallah Jamma, whose descendants, the Abdallab, ruled the northern part of the kingdom from Qerri as vassals of Sennar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funj-sultanate">Funj Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Admitting that the <b>White Nile</b> was longer, he argued that the Blue Nile was the Nile of the ancients.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-14">14 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Abdallah established his seat at Qerri, north of the confluence of the Blue and <b>White Niles</b>, controlling the routes north toward Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/state/abdallab-sheikhdom">Abdallab Sheikhdom</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Shilluk tradition, the kingdom was founded by Nyikang, who led his people north along the <b>White Nile</b> and settled them on its west bank.</blockquote><a href="/state/shilluk-kingdom">Shilluk Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Merchants opened the <b>White Nile</b> and the south to the ivory and slave trades, with private armies such as that of Zubayr's Bahr al-Ghazal.</blockquote><a href="/state/turco-egyptian-sudan">Turco-Egyptian Sudan</a></li></ul></section>
